  ["How hard is WSET Level 3?", "It is a big step from Level 2: the syllabus covers the main wine regions of the world in depth, the written answers ask you to explain causes and the tasting is assessed blind. It is very achievable with regular study, daily practice questions, written answers marked on key points and plenty of tasting with full notes."],
  ["How many hours should I study for WSET Level 3?", "The indicated total is about 84 hours: 32.5 hours of guided learning, including the exams, and 51.5 hours of private study. Many candidates spread the work over 10 to 12 weeks; aiming for Merit or Distinction usually means more private study, especially written answers and tasting."],
  ["Do I need Level 2 before Level 3?", "It is not compulsory, but Level 2 or an equivalent level of knowledge is recommended. If you go straight to Level 3, make sure you already know the main grapes, regions and label terms, and discuss your level with your course provider before enrolling."],
  ["What is the pass mark for WSET Level 3?", "You need at least 55% in each part of the theory unit, the multiple-choice questions and the written answers, and at least 55% in the tasting unit. An overall 65% gives a Merit, and 80% with at least 65% in each paper a Distinction."],
  ["How long is the WSET Level 3 exam?", "The theory paper lasts two hours for 50 multiple-choice questions and four written questions worth 25 marks each. The tasting lasts 30 minutes for two still wines. Both units are sat in the same session and in the same language."],
  ["Can I resit only one unit?", "Yes. If you pass one unit and fail the other, you resit only the failed unit, in the same language. There is no limit on the number of resits, but a resit candidate can reach a Merit at most."],
  ["Do I have to identify the wines in the tasting?", "No. You describe two still wines with the Level 3 Systematic Approach to Tasting and reach conclusions on quality and readiness for drinking. Precise structural levels, relevant descriptors and consistent conclusions earn the marks."],
